// zKolang virtual machine gadgets, so a zKolang program can verify a zKolang execution: the
// language checking a run of itself. A step of the register machine selects one operation by a
// one-hot opcode and produces its result on the operands, exactly as the step AIR gates the
// opcodes. `step_result` is that gated result; `is_onehot3` is zero when the selectors name one
// operation. Assert each selector a bit, assert is_onehot3, and bind the result to step_result,
// and a trace of these is a proof that a run followed the machine's rules.

// The result of the selected operation, add or subtract or multiply the operands. Exact when
// the selectors are a one-hot triple, the same one-hot-times-op gating the step AIR uses.
fn step_result(is_add, is_sub, is_mul, a, b) =
    is_add * (a + b) + is_sub * (a - b) + is_mul * (a * b);

// Zero exactly when three bit selectors sum to one, so a step names exactly one operation.
fn is_onehot3(is_add, is_sub, is_mul) = (is_add + is_sub + is_mul) - 1;
